# First Steps

> **Draft status:** this is the future v1 tutorial. The toolbar, card and
> Protein instructions below describe the legacy interface that can be used
> today. Sections explicitly marked **planned native Box** explain the accepted
> replacement and must lose that label only after a person can perform the
> described work in the running application.

Open Lince and there is no menu bar, no sidebar, no home screen waiting to be navigated. There is a canvas — a large surface you pan and zoom — and on it, small tools called **sands**: a table, a kanban, a record editor, a terminal, a graph of relations, a transfer panel, a rule builder, a reader for this very document. You choose which ones exist, where they sit, how big they are, and — the part that matters most — what data each one is looking at. Nothing here is a fixed screen, because nobody's work is a fixed screen.

The frame around the canvas is turning into sands as well. Some of it already is: every new workspace comes with an **Edit** panel pinned to the side, which is a sand like any other and can be moved, resized or thrown away. The rest — the small toolbar in the corner with the pencil and the notification bell, the zoom controls beside it — is still drawn by the host. The direction is one Lince rather than an application with a canvas inside it, so that the parts you would normally call "the program" end up the same kind of thing as the parts you build for yourself.

A **workspace** is one canvas with its arrangement of sands. Keep several, name them, switch between them, throw one away when it has served its purpose — nothing underneath is lost, because a sand is a window onto your records, never a box holding them. The same record can appear on four boards at once and it is still one record.

The ideas below walk through the frontend in order: the toolbar in the corner, what edit mode turns on, the two layers a sand can live in, how to add one, and then **Protein** — the thing that decides which records a sand is looking at, which is where the frontend stops being about windows and starts being about your data.

The native interface is still a developer preview rather than the finished
Box described later in this chapter. Run `mise run interface-lab-joined` to
try the parts that have landed. Press **F9** for primitive Sand states,
**F10** for recursive Sand composition, **F11** for the Configuration Sand and
**F12** for the official-Sand migration catalog.
In either workbench, use **Tab** and **Shift-Tab** to select an operation and
**Enter** to apply it; pointer and accessibility activation reach the same
operation. Press **F8** to write the machine-readable report.

Configuration currently lets you exercise Dark/Light and theme selection,
typed workspace, group and instance values, inheritance, definition defaults,
ports and Behavior, isolation and capabilities, safe developer CSS, undo,
save/reopen and an idempotent domain-compound launch. Each accepted operation
previews immediately and the panel shows where representative resolved values
came from. A refused operation says so and keeps the last good preview. This
F-key surface is the implementation laboratory, not the intended final route
through the v1 Configuration Sand; it is documented so a person can test what
exists while the official interface is rebuilt.

The F12 catalog lists all 25 official Sands. Use Tab or Shift-Tab, click a
name, or select it through accessibility controls. The panel shows which Rust
definitions compose that Sand, its typed inputs and outputs, and the legacy
source whose behavior still has to be preserved. Press Enter or Space on Edit
controls, Zoom controls, Record or Conversation to open its native retained runtime.
Inside it, Tab and Shift-Tab move among its real composed controls; Enter,
Space, a pointer click or accessibility activation operates the focused Sand.
Record's textarea accepts typing and Backspace. Escape returns to the catalog.

`Landed` means the complete native behavior is usable. Edit, Zoom, Record,
Conversation, Table, Todo and Kanban now have native retained Behavior. When F12 is reached through
the production desktop, Record and Conversation read the running Lince through
live Protein subscriptions; the panel says whether that connection is live and
how many Records, Conversations and private drafts arrived. The laboratory
without a domain endpoint uses clearly labeled representative data.

Conversation shows the latest Message's author, the Person or Agent that
operated for that author, its words and whether it is still `writing`,
`finished` or `interrupted`. Its composer and draft queue are private working
state. Type in the textarea; **Add draft** creates another entry, **Pinned**
turns one into a reusable preset, **Move up** changes queue order and **Delete**
removes it. Changes wait briefly so typing does not write on every key, then
persist through Lince Actions and return after restart. **Send** first saves an
unsaved draft. It removes an ordinary draft only after the Message is
acknowledged and keeps a pinned preset. If no Fiote turn is running, the timing
control honestly says “send now · no turn running”; safe-point and after-turn
delivery become active only when a running turn is attached.

Table shows one reusable row Sand for every Record on its current page. Todo
shows plain Records whose quantity is negative: type a title into **New task**,
choose **Add task**, or choose **Complete** to request quantity zero. Kanban
places Records into Backlog, Next, WIP, Review and Done from their quantity.
Choose **Open Record** on a card to select it, then **Move left** or **Move
right**. These operations wait for Lince to acknowledge the Action; live
Protein then decides what remains on screen.

This is the first collection behavior, not its final editor. Table cell editing
and deletion, custom Protein drivers, custom Kanban lanes and concepts, bulk
selection and swimlanes still use the old Web Sand while C4 continues.

`Rust structure cataloged; runtime Behavior pending` means only the decomposed
shape is ready; the old Web Sand is still the usable implementation. Seventeen
official roots currently have that state. This distinction keeps the tutorial
honest while the migration proceeds.

Everything after this branch is what sits underneath. First why Lince exists at all, then the model everything is made from, then the boundary between one Lince and another, the transfers that cross it, and the rules that keep working while you are not looking.

## Sands on a canvas

The frontend is the part of Lince you can touch: a large zoomable canvas and many small tools called **sands**. A sand can be a table, a kanban, a record editor, a terminal, a document viewer, a transfer panel, a relations graph, or anything else that fits in a widget.

Nothing here is a fixed screen. You choose which sands exist, where they sit, how big they are, and — this is the important part — **what data each one is looking at**.

## The triangle in the corner

Look at the bottom-right corner of the canvas. That small grey triangle is the board's own toolbar, collapsed. Hover it (or reach it with the keyboard) and it opens into a row of controls:

- **− / % / +** — zoom out, current zoom, zoom in. Clicking the percentage snaps you back to 100%.

- **⌖ Recenter** — brings the viewport back over your sands when you have wandered off into empty canvas.

- **◎ Reorganize** — gathers the loose sands back toward the centre.

- **The pencil** — toggles **edit mode**. This is the switch everything else depends on.

The triangle stays out of your way on purpose: in normal use the canvas should be the workspace, not a cockpit. If you would rather have those controls always visible, the catalog also ships them as sands ("Lince Zoom" and "Lince Edit") that you can add and pin.

## Edit mode

With the pencil off you are in **Dashboard** mode: sands are live and you interact with their contents. With it on you are in **Edit mode**, and you arrange the workspace itself instead of using it.

## What edit mode turns on

- A floating **+ Add card** button and a **Grid** density slider appear over the canvas.

- Every sand grows drag-and-resize handles on its edges and corners.

- Every sand grows a small toolbar: configure (the gear), pin, layer order, download HTML, and delete.

- Ctrl-drag (⌘ on a Mac) draws a selection box, so you can move or group several sands at once.

## Pinned vs. canvas

- **Canvas sands** live in the zoomable world. They move and scale with the board.

- **Pinned sands** are stuck to the screen and ignore zoom — that is where navigation, status and always-on tools belong.

- The pin button on a sand's toolbar moves it between the two layers.

- Turn edit mode off when you are done. Handles and toolbars disappear and the sands become usable again.

## Putting sands on the canvas

Turn on edit mode, then use **+ Add card**. It offers two routes:

- **Add card** — pick from the sand catalog: the official sands shipped with Lince plus anything you have installed.

- **Importar** — load a widget from disk (`.html`, `.sand` or `.lince`). You can also just drag the file onto the canvas; it is read by the backend and shown in preview before it becomes a card.

A new sand lands on the canvas where there is room. Drag it, resize it from any edge or corner, and use the Grid slider if you want it to snap to a coarser or finer grid.

## Protein: how a sand gets data

**Protein** is Lince's query. It answers "which records should this card show?" — and it is a live subscription, not a one-off fetch: when the underlying records change, every card driven by that Protein redraws by itself.

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Records in your Cell"] --> B["Protein: filter, include, sort, limit"]
  B --> C["A sand: kanban, table, graph..."]
```

_A sand never reaches for records directly. A Protein stands between them, and you own it._

To set one, turn on edit mode, click the **gear** on a sand, and find the **Data** section. You get the list of saved Proteins, an explicit **All records** entry, and **+ New Protein** to build one with dropdowns. There is no query language to type.

**A new sand shows nothing until you pick a Protein — that is deliberate.** Cards do not default to "everything". In an organ that has accumulated thousands of records, a card that defaulted to all of them would dump the entire database into a freshly-added board and be useless on arrival. So a brand-new card subscribes to **nothing** and renders a short prompt instead. _No data source configured_ and _All records_ are two different states: the second is a choice you make on purpose.

## What you can put in a Protein

- **Show** — records, promises, or decisions.

- **Filters** — kind, slug, uid, concept, organ, quantity comparisons, state, links, assignee. Add as many as you need; they combine.

- **Include extra info** — history (facts), promises, availability, and links of one or more kinds. Sands ignore what they do not use, so including more is safe.

- **Aggregate** — sum or count, grouped by concept or kind, when you want totals instead of rows.

- **Sort** — high → low, low → high, or graph order following a link kind.

- **Limit** — cap the number of rows.

Give it a name and save it. Saved Proteins are records themselves, so the same query can drive several cards, and editing it updates all of them at once.

## Areas make an interface from data — planned native Box

A normal application gives you screens chosen by its developer. Box instead
lets you place a few reusable **Areas of influence** around Sands fed by
Protein. Together those Areas can behave like many different applications.

- A **Protein Area** is where matching data enters. You choose a Protein and
  build one small group of Sands as its template. Each result becomes another
  complete instance of that group.

- A **force Area** attracts or repels matching groups. It can gather urgent
  work, push completed work away, or carry items through a visual process.

- A **sorting Area** puts the groups inside it in a declared order and
  direction. A few bounded sorting Areas can become Kanban columns.

- A **mutation Area** previews an Action when a matching group enters. It does
  nothing permanent until you explicitly arm it, and every accepted change is
  still an attributed Lince change rather than anonymous physics.

- An **immunity Area** protects the results from one Protein Area against
  influences outside its boundary while keeping influences inside valid.

The whole locked result group moves as one object. A title Sand cannot be torn
away from the quantity button and assignee Sands it was packaged with. Select
**Why is it here?** to see the source Protein, field arrows, matching Areas,
forces, sorting, collisions and Actions that explain a group's current place.

This is the larger idea behind the interface: a Kanban, inventory line, case
pipeline, dashboard or small simulation should be a useful arrangement of
shared building blocks, not another isolated application with another private
copy of your data.

## Surface, topology and free space — planned native Box

Box has two spatial modes. It also has camera controls, and those are not the
same choice.

### Surface mode: cards always touch the floor

Surface mode begins like a normal 2D canvas. In edit mode you can sculpt that
canvas with raise, lower, flatten, smooth, circle, square and ridge brushes. A
pit pulls matching groups downhill; a hill pushes them away; a slope can carry
them along a production line. Filters mean two kinds of Record may experience
different terrain at the same place, so the field lens tells you whose surface
you are looking at.

Choose **Top** to look straight down. Choose **Perspective** to tilt or orbit
the camera and see the same plane in 3D. Cards are glued to the surface: they
sit at its height, follow its slope and always touch their effective floor.
Their text can still face you on a readable panel. Top and Perspective do not
change the simulation or move the data; they are two ways to inspect it.

### Space mode: cards float and Areas become volumes

Choose **Space** only when you intend to change the physics. The topology floor
stops supporting or moving Sands, and every world Sand can move in `x`, `y`
and `z`. Force Areas become three-dimensional volumes that gather or repel
matching Records. Sorting Areas use a direction or a local shelf. Mutation and
immunity Areas react when a group enters their volume. Ordinary cards remain
readable instead of spinning uncontrollably, while a Sand designed as a 3D
object may expose rotation.

A thin ghost plane marks local `z = 0`. It is not a floor and cannot catch a
Sand. It shows where things would land if the workspace were collapsed back to
the surface. Box can draw an Area's footprint on it and a line from a selected
floating item to its projected position.

Moving from Surface to Space keeps every `x` and `y`, starts each Sand at the
height of the terrain it was touching, and expands flat Areas into volumes.
Moving back previews an overhead projection: each `(x, y, z)` becomes `(x, y)`,
Area volumes become footprints, and Sands touch their effective terrain again.
You see overlaps before committing. The conversion happens as one undoable
edit, so undo restores the exact prior transforms.

Remember the two controls:

- **Top / Perspective** changes the camera and never changes physics.
- **Surface / Space** changes the simulation and always previews the
  conversion.

## One card for Project A, one for Project B

This is the common case: your tasks all live in the same organ, but you want a board per project. It turns on one choice — whether a word stands alone, or points at a record.

## A concept on its own

A word applied to a record and nothing more: `@task`, `@urgent`. Good for qualities, bad for projects — one word per project means inventing `@project-a`, `@project-b`, and the projects themselves stay imaginary.

## A concept naming a record

The same word, plus the record it points at — a **link**. Project A becomes a real record you can open, count and rename, and a task can belong to several things at once.

```mermaid
graph LR
  T1["Task: write the spec"] -- tag --> PA["Project A"]
  T2["Task: fix the bug"] -- tag --> PA
  T3["Task: call the client"] -- tag --> PB["Project B"]
```

_Same pile of tasks, two boards — the link kind `tag` is what tells them apart._

Concretely, in order:

- Make a plain record named **Project A** (with an Organ or Record sand). Note its slug — probably `project-a`. Do the same for **Project B**.

- Open a task in the **Record** sand and go to its **Links** section. Type `tag` as the kind, `project-a` as the target, and press **Link**. Repeat for the other tasks — link kinds need no setup, the first link creates the vocabulary word.

- Add a kanban (or table) card, turn on edit mode, open its gear → **Data** → **+ New Protein**. Name it "Project A tasks".

- Add a filter **Kind is** → `task`, then a second filter **Relation** — kind `tag`, direction **outgoing**, value `project-a`. Save.

- Add a second card and repeat with `project-b`. Two boards, same records underneath, no duplication.

Two things trip people up here. The Record sand calls it a **link** and the Data panel calls it a **Relation** — same thing, two words. And the kind box autocompletes from vocabulary you already use, so `tag` only appears in the list _after_ the first link exists. Create the link first, then build the Protein.

**Assignee** is the same filter with the ceremony removed: kind `assigned-to`, pre-filled, with a person picker. And because filters combine, "Project A but not archived", or "assigned to me across both projects", are just more filters on the same card.

That is the current legacy frontend plus the accepted shape of the native Box.
As the native controls become usable, this draft will replace old toolbar and
card instructions instead of keeping two tutorials forever. The chapters that
follow are about what is underneath it — starting with the one thing everything
in Lince is made of.
